-- title:
--   Pole-chart points in the finite chart: criterion via 1/j
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, let $F$ be a field carrying an $R$-algebra structure, and let $j \in F$ be nonzero. Write $A_\infty =$ `TwoChartIntegralModel.chartAlgInf R F j` for the $R$-subalgebra of $F$ consisting of those elements of $F$ that are integral over the $R$-subalgebra of $F$ generated by $j^{-1}$, and let `TwoChartIntegralModel.jInvChartInf R F j` denote the element $j^{-1}$ of $A_\infty$. The two-chart integral model [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel R F j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) is the pushout, in the category of schemes, of the two morphisms `fFin` and `fInf` obtained by applying $\operatorname{Spec}$ to the inclusions of the middle chart algebra into the finite and the pole chart algebras, and `ιFin`, `ιInf` are the resulting morphisms from the finite and the pole chart into this pushout. The assertion is that for every prime ideal $\mathfrak q$ of $A_\infty$, the point `(ιInf R F j).base 𝔮` of the two-chart integral model lies in the image of the underlying continuous map of `ιFin R F j` if and only if $j^{-1} \notin \mathfrak q$.
--
--   This identifies the overlap of the two charts of the integral model from the side of the pole chart: the points of $\operatorname{Spec} A_\infty$ that are also visible in the finite chart are exactly those in the basic open set where $j^{-1}$ is invertible, so that the complement of the finite chart is the locus $j = \infty$. It is used in counting irreducible components of a pullback of the model, and in the study of the model of a modular curve, where it decides in which chart a given point lives.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel_iotaInf_mem_range_iotaFin_iff.lean

